    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "going to push on that. But my optimism related to the budget is just not very optimistic, honestly. So we're back to the COLA. So the cost of living adjustment, the governor led with her executive budget. And this is a couple of months ago now. with a 2.5% increase and her justification is that we've had to increases. But as we all know, inflation has made things more challenging. We've had to compete in the job market, which means that we have to enhance salaries for our individuals who are working directly with individuals with disabilities. And as a result, those challenges are great. So we need to support that. And I was happy to be a leader on the Senate side to support an 8.5% cost of living adjustment. And they did the same in the assembly. There is now, we are in the midst of negotiations and not to go on another tangent, but there's a lot of conversations and I won't go into too much detail related to criminal justice reform related to our transition to a cleaner, greener energy system. and housing and those are just a few things. So the good news is that the advocacy for people with developmental disabilities is very strong. I do believe we have the attention of my leadership and the governor and leadership in the assembly that we really have to support this community significantly because of the challenges that have grown even greater over the last decade. So I am hopeful that, you know, that we get somewhere significantly above 5%. I do not have any insight information, but I will tell you that it's not just the OPWDD quote unquote community that's involved. It involves all of the O agencies. So I'm partnering with Senator Brooke, who's the chair of mental health. So she is advocating for an 8.5% increase for OMH. Senator Fernandez is our new chair this year of substance abuse, so she is pushing for a significant increase for OASIS. We have Office of the Aging and Office of Children and Family",
